  "narrative": "The path is confirmed: α-D-Galactose → UDP-D-Galactose → D-Glucose, establishing a bidirectional reachable relationship in the Leloir pathway. I now have sufficient evidence to write the narrative.\n\n---\n\nThe differential metabolite panel is most strongly associated with **galactose metabolism** (KEGG map00052; FDR=0.0, fold-enrichment=950.9) and its disease counterpart **galactosemia** (SMP00182; FDR=0.0, fold-enrichment=982.6). Six metabolites anchor this finding: **sucrose (C00089)**, **D-glucose (C00221)**, **myo-inositol (C00137)**, **glycerol (C00116)**, **galactinol (C01235)**, and **galactitol (C01697)** — all confirmed members of both pathways via the KEGG and SMPDB pathway membership databases.\n\n**Key driver compounds.** Galactitol (C01697) and galactinol (C01235) are particularly noteworthy because they are near-terminal markers of the Leloir pathway: galactitol is the sorbitol analogue produced by aldose reductase from galactose, and its accumulation is the primary toxic metabolite in classic galactosemia. Galactinol, the galactosyl-donor for raffinose-family oligosaccharide synthesis, reflects upstream Leloir flux. **D-glucose (C00221)**, a confirmed downstream product of the Leloir pathway (α-D-galactose → UDP-D-galactose → D-glucose; KEGG bidirectional path length=2), is also perturbed, consistent with redirecting carbon flow away from glucose and into galactose-derived polyol and raffinose-family pools.\n\n**Biological significance.** Coordinated dysregulation of these six metabolites is consistent with impaired galactose-to-glucose interconversion — the hallmark of galactosemia — where galactose accumulates and shunts into galactitol and galactinol synthesis. The simultaneous elevation or depletion of these intermediates relative to controls suggests the treatment group may model an acute or chronic perturbation of galactose handling. The concurrent detection of **acetaminophen (C06804)**, **aspirin (C01405)**, and **N-hydroxy-4-aminobiphenyl (C20319)** points to an additional **drug/foreign-compound metabolism** axis (Reactome R-HSA-9749641, FDR=2×10⁻⁶), which may reflect exposure or therapeutic intervention interacting with the underlying carbohydrate metabolism defect.",
